  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCurrently-Thursday... KMLB radar imagery shows scattered showers \nand lightning storms mainly across the Treasure Coast which have \nproduce some gusty winds to around 30-40mph. GOES-16 satellite \nimagery and local observations indicate partly to mostly cloudy \nskies across east central Florida. Analysis charts show a \nmid/upper low over the northeastern US with a shortwave trough \nover northern Florida. Meanwhile, the Atlantic ridge axis is well \nto the east- Southeast of east central Florida. There's a wide \nrange of current temperatures currently across east central due to\nrain cooled air over much of the Treasure Coast to Okeechobee \ncounty; temperatures are in the low 80s to mid 90s. Dew points are\nin the mid 70s to around 80 degrees with heat index values \ngenerally between 100F-108F degrees (hottest across the north). \nWinds are breezy from the west- southwest at 10-20mph with gusts \nto 20-25mph.\n\nIsolated to scattered slow generally east moving showers (30-50% \nchance) and lightning storms are forecast this afternoon and \nevening with the greatest chances to the south of the Orlando \nMetro. The main hazards with this afternoon and evening lightning \nstorms are occasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts to \n40-60mph, and heavy rainfall (up to 1-3\"+ in 30-60 mins). A line \nof scattered to numerous showers (30-70% chance) and storms are \nforecast to move south-southeast across east central Florida \nmainly after 4AM across areas north of I-4 before tracking \nsouthwards and reaching the Treasure Coast by the afternoon hours.\nGuidance is not in good agreement on the timing, as well as the \ncoverage of showers, especially the CAMs. However, they have come \ninto better agreement than yesterday with the FV3 and NAMNest \nshowing a solution closer to the HRRR. \n\nA Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe storms has been \noutlooked over far northern Lake county, as well as Volusia \ncounties through 8AM Thursday. The potential exists for isolated \nstrong to severe storms capable of wind gusts up to 60mph, \nfrequent lightning, and heavy rainfall (up to 2-4\" with a 1 in 10 \nchance in excess of 4\" up to 4-6\") which has the potential to \nresult in flooding. For this reason, a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall (5-14% chance) exists on Thursday. Afternoon highs in the\nupper 80s to low 90s and lows in the mid to upper 70s are \nforecast.\n\nFriday-Saturday... A similar forecast is expected on Friday with a\nstationary boundary over the Deep South and weak low pressure over\nGeorgia. However confidence in the timing of east- southeast \nmoving scattered to numerous showers and lightning storms is \nrather low. The greatest chance (50-70%) for scattered to numerous\nshowers and storms is Friday morning into Friday afternoon. \nHowever, global models and CAMs disagree again on the timing with \nthe CAMs mostly not showing much overnight into Friday morning and\nthe global models showing a scenario similar to tonight's. \nGuidance indicates MLCAPE up to 3,000 J/kg on Friday, 0-6km shear\nup to 20-40kts, areas of PVA traversing ECFL, and a LLJ up to \n25kts. The potential exists for strong to severe storms on Friday \n(greatest potential across northern ECFL). Scattered to numerous \nshowers (60-70% chance) and lightning storms are forecast on \nSaturday. Winds will remain gusts from the west- southwest \ngenerally at 10-15mph with gusts to 20-25mph. Additionally, the \npotential exists for strong storms on Saturday. Near normal highs \nand above normal lows are expected.\n\nSunday-Wednesday... The active weather pattern is expected to\ncontinue on Sunday with a mid/upper level trough over the \nsouthern US. WSW flow weakens into next week which will allow the\neast coast sea breeze to develop on Monday before flow becomes\neasterly Tuesday and Wednesday. The NBM probability of \nprecipitation (PoPs) remains high through Sunday with near to \nbelow normal rain chances Monday through Wednesday as drier air \nfilters in over east central Florida. Scattered to numerous \nshowers (60%-70% chance) and lightning storms are forecast on \nSunday with rain chances gradually diminishing into next week. \nNear to slightly below normal high temperatures are forecast with \nnear to above normal low temperatures through Monday with slightly\nwarmer temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday.",
